//! update_watch — the update check on an operator-named cadence.
//!
//! The embedded update-check document is manual-only by design: the server
//! never phones home on its own. This document does not change that law —
//! it IS the explicit operator act, stretched over time. Nothing schedules
//! this workflow either; the operator starts ONE run and names, as demanded
//! inputs, how often it checks and how many checks it makes. When the run
//! completes, the checking stops until an operator starts another. A longer
//! watch is another run, started with its own named parameters.
//!
//! THE CHECK ITSELF IS THE EMBEDDED CHECK, NOT A COPY. The server's
//! update-status observer recognises a check by its task queue, its action
//! name, and the resolved command — verbatim against the constants in
//! `crates/aion-server/src/update_check/document.rs` — not by workflow
//! type. This document declares exactly that queue, that action, and that
//! command, so every cycle's completed fetch is recorded by the same
//! observer that records a console "check now", and `GET /update-status`
//! (and the console's update pill) refreshes on each cycle. An edit here
//! that drifts the command from the constant produces fetches the observer
//! IGNORES — visibly, because the pill's `checked_at` stops moving.
//!
//! The cadence is a demanded input spent as `pace(seconds: ...)` — the
//! language's durable `sleep` takes only duration literals, and a literal
//! cadence would be an authored value the operator never named. The check
//! count is a demanded input spent as the flow-loop visit bound; "never
//! emulate an unbounded loop" is the language's law. 48 × 1800 s = one day
//! of half-hourly checks.
//!
//! WHAT IT NEVER DOES: no install, no upgrade, no restart — the same line
//! the embedded check draws. Knowing a newer aion exists and becoming it
//! are different acts, and neither this document nor the check takes the
//! second one.
workflow update_watch
  input interval_seconds: Int
  input checks: Int
  query last_check: Fetched
  outcome completed: type WatchReport, route success
  outcome refused:   type Refused,     route failure
  outcome died:      type Refused,     route failure

// The declared-body executor's whole result — the same record the embedded
// update-check document declares, kept field-for-field so the recorded
// activity result carries exactly what the fetch answered.
type Fetched { exit_code: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String }

/// What a completed watch reports: the parameters it was named with and its
/// final check. Every check's result is in the run's history.
type WatchReport { checks_requested: Int, interval_seconds: Int, last_check: Fetched }

/// A run that did not watch, said out loud rather than reported as a pass.
type Refused { reason: String }

worker update_check
  /// One GET of the sparse-index line file for `aion-cli` — VERBATIM the
  /// embedded check's command, because the observer records a result only
  /// from this exact body on this exact queue and action.
  action fetch_crate_index() -> Fetched
    run "curl -fsS https://index.crates.io/ai/on/aion-cli"

worker update_watch
  /// The cadence, spent as a parameterized sleep through the declared-body
  /// spine: `sleep` resolved via PATH, the demanded seconds its one argument.
  action pace(seconds: Int) -> Paced
    run "sleep $seconds"

/// The pace leg's outcome record subset: its exit status, nothing more.
type Paced { exit_code: Int }

/// Refuse out-of-range values by name before anything runs. The engine has
/// already refused a start missing any input; this step refuses one that
/// names a value no run could mean.
step validate
  outcome named: when interval_seconds >= 1 and checks >= 1,
    route check
  outcome unnamed: otherwise,
    route refused(reason: "refusing to start: interval_seconds and checks must both be >= 1. There are no defaults; the values are the operator's to name.")

step check
  fetch_crate_index() -> fetched
  answer last_check(fetched)
  on failure
    route died(reason: "an update check died — curl exited non-zero (network down, crates.io unreachable, or an HTTP error status). The leg's own stderr is in the run history; nothing was retried silently.")

step decide
  outcome more: when visits < checks, route pace
  outcome finished: otherwise,
    route completed(checks_requested: checks, interval_seconds: interval_seconds, last_check: fetched)
  max checks visits

step pace
  pace(seconds: interval_seconds)
  route check
